{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":13286252,"ns":0,"title":"Graphism thesis","extract":"In the sociology of science and science and technology studies (STS), the graphism thesis is a proposition advanced by Bruno Latour that visual inscriptions—especially graphs, diagrams, and other visual representations—are central to the practice and authority of science. First articulated in Latour's 1990 essay \"Visualization and Cognition: Drawing Things Together\", the thesis holds that the power of scientific disciplines is closely linked to their capacity to produce, circulate, and combine visual representations of data.","description":"Proposition that visual inscriptions are central to scientific practice","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}